[medium] Null validation is missing: the constructor never checks whether the `purposes` parameter or its elements are null. That can surface as a NullReferenceException later, when `GetByteCount` or `GetBytes` is called. Consider validating at the start of the constructor:

```csharp
ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(purposes);
for (int i = 0; i < purposes.Length; i++)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(purposes[i], $"purposes[{i}]");
}
```

Alternatively, at minimum add `Debug.Assert` statements in the spirit of the original implementation.
